---
/**
 * LAYR 3, the compiled UI language. The 1.x React component library this
 * page used to describe is covered under "LAYR 1.x".
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../components/AdSlot.astro";

const SITE = "https://layr.dynshift.com/";
const NPM = "https://www.npmjs.com/package/@dynshift/layr";
const REPO = "https://github.com/nishanbhuinya/layr";
---

<Base
  title="LAYR"
  description="LAYR is a compiled UI language for the web: layout written in your design's own pixels, compiled to static CSS and React. What it is for, what is in it, and how it relates to the 1.x React library."
>
  <section class="wrap prose">
    <p class="meta muted">Project · Web, CSS + React · MIT · Version 3.3.0, October 2026</p>
    <h1>LAYR</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Layout Authoritative Yet Responsive: a compiled language for building
      websites deterministically, in your design's own numbers.
    </p>
    <p class="links">
      <a class="btn" href={SITE}>layr.dynshift.com</a>
      <a href={NPM} rel="noopener">@dynshift/layr on npm</a>
      <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Building a page from a design is mostly translation. The design says 390
      pixels wide, a 24-pixel gap, a 16-pixel radius; the code says rems,
      viewport units, <code>clamp()</code> and media queries, plus a decision at
      every breakpoint. By the end, the stylesheet no longer looks like the page,
      and nobody can say exactly why the layout behaves as it does at 700 pixels.
    </p>
    <p>
      LAYR makes the layout the program. A <code>.layr</code> file describes what
      the page is, objects inside objects, so the code has the shape of the
      result. Numbers are pixels of the design frame. A compiler checks the whole
      thing before it runs and turns it into static CSS and React components.
    </p>

    <h2>What is in it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The language.</strong> Pages, widgets, state and functions, with
        logic kept to clearly marked places so the tree of objects stays readable.
        One canonical form: <code>layr format</code> writes every file the same way.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Design Scale.</strong> Design frames for phone, tablet, desktop and
        ultrawide; values scale with the screen inside a frame and are interpolated
        between frames, all compiled to plain CSS. No media queries to write.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deterministic layout.</strong> Fixed, hug and fill sizing, and a
        documented order of adaptation when space runs out: shrink, then wrap or
        stack, and clip only with a warning. <code>layr analyze</code> explains any
        adaptation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>EIED.</strong> Export, Inject, Extract and Duplicate let any part
        of an app read, change or copy any object, as ordered, reversible layers
        the compiler checks. Objects are protected by default, and
        <code>!mut</code> protects what must never change.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tooling.</strong> The <code>layr</code> CLI, guided upgrades, a
        language server and a VS Code extension.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Built for agents as well as people</h2>
    <p>
      LAYR is designed to be written by AI coding agents as well as by people.
      Where CSS offers many ways to write the same layout, LAYR has one canonical
      form; layout follows fixed, documented rules instead of being found out in
      a browser; and every problem is a coded diagnostic that
      <code>layr analyze --json</code> reports in a form an agent can act on.
      <code>layr skills install</code> adds the LAYR Skill, generated from the
      compiler's own schema and matched to the installed version, and an MCP
      server that can look up widgets, check a snippet and analyse the project.
    </p>

    <AdSlot />

    <h2>How it fits with React</h2>
    <p>
      LAYR compiles to React, and works with it in both directions: React packages
      can be used inside LAYR, even as JSX in the tree, and an existing React app
      can import <code>.layr</code> files. React is the engine underneath, not the
      way LAYR is written, so a project can adopt it one page at a time.
    </p>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      People who build websites from designs and want the design's numbers to be
      the code's numbers: designers who write their own front ends, developers who
      work closely with design files, and small teams who would rather read a
      layout than reverse-engineer one. Flutter developers will recognise the
      object tree and the hug-and-fill sizing.
    </p>

    <h2>LAYR 1.x</h2>
    <p>
      Until September 2026, <code>@dynshift/layr</code> was a small library of
      Flutter-style layout components for React (<code>Container</code>,
      <code>Row</code>, <code>Column</code>, <code>Stack</code> and the rest).
      LAYR 3.0 replaced it as the default package on 25 September 2026. The 1.x
      library stays installable as <code>@dynshift/layr@v1</code>, its source is
      on the repository's <code>v1</code> branch, and the documentation has a
      guide for migrating.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting it</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={`${SITE}playground`}>playground</a> runs LAYR in the browser with
      nothing to install. For a project, install <code>@dynshift/layr</code> from
      npm and run <code>layr create</code>. The <a href={SITE}>LAYR site</a> has
      the documentation, the widget reference and the changelog; the source,
      issues and pull requests are on <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
      Two articles go further:
      <a href="/articles/introducing-layr/">Introducing LAYR</a> and
      <a href="/articles/how-layr-works/">How LAYR works</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section {
    padding-block: clamp(24px, 5vw, 56px);
  }
  .meta {
    margin-bottom: 1.2em;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px 24px;
    margin: 1.4em 0 1em;
  }
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 11px 18px;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--ground);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .btn:hover {
    background: #fff;
  }
  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
